Aim

To attach an EBS volume to a Linux EC2 instance and permanently mount it so that data persists across reboots.

Procedure
Step 1: Create EBS Volume
Open EC2 Dashboard.
Go to:
Volumes → Create Volume
Configure:
Type: gp3
Size: 20 GB
Same Availability Zone as EC2
Click Create Volume.
Step 2: Attach Volume
Select created volume.
Click:
Actions → Attach Volume
Choose EC2 instance.

Device name:

/dev/xvdf
Click Attach.
Step 3: Connect to EC2

Use SSH:

ssh -i key.pem ec2-user@public-ip
Step 4: Verify Disk

Run:

lsblk

New disk appears as:

/dev/nvme1n1
Step 5: Create Partition

Run:

sudo fdisk /dev/nvme1n1

Inside fdisk:

Press n
Press p
Press Enter for defaults
Press w

Then run:

sudo partprobe
Step 6: Verify Partition

Run:

lsblk

Partition appears as:

/dev/nvme1n1p1
Step 7: Format Partition

Run:

sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme1n1p1
Step 8: Create Mount Directory

Run:

sudo mkdir /mnt/archana
Step 9: Mount Volume

Run:

sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/archana

Verify:

df -h
Step 10: Make Mount Permanent

Get UUID:

sudo blkid /dev/nvme1n1p1

Edit fstab:

sudo nano /etc/fstab

Add line:

UUID=<uuid-value> /mnt/archana xfs defaults,nofail 0 0

Save file.

Step 11: Test Persistent Mount

Run:

sudo mount -a

Create test file:

cd /mnt/archana
touch f1
Step 12: Reboot and Verify

Run:

sudo reboot

Reconnect and verify:

df -h
Observation
EBS volume attached successfully.
Partition created and formatted.
Volume mounted successfully.
Data remained available after reboot due to persistent mounting using /etc/fstab.
Result

Successfully attached and permanently mounted an EBS volume to a Linux EC2 instance ensuring data persistence across reboots.
